<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Dilmaçoğulları</h1><p class="st-meta">1085 CE – 1192 CE (approx.) · capital Bitlis</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A minor Turkish dynasty that ruled Bitlis and Erzen in south-eastern Anatolia in the 12th century.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Known mainly from brief mentions in Arabic chronicles, the dynasty descended from a Turkish commander named Dilmac who received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bitlis">Bitlis</a> and Erzen, south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-van">Lake Van</a>, after the Seljuk conquest of the region in the late 11th century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its best-documented ruler is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toghan-arslan">Toghan Arslan</a>, nicknamed the Hunchback, who held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bitlis">Bitlis</a> and Erzen in the early 12th century, allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artuqid-dynasty">Artuqid</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilghazi">Ilghazi</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgians">Georgians</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a>, and fought his neighbours over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mayyafariqin">Mayyafariqin</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beylik</a> controlled the pass between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-van">Lake Van</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> basin and acted as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of stronger neighbours.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The dynasty faded under the pressure of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/shah-armens">Shah-Armens</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahlat">Ahlat</a> and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubids</a>; its lands were absorbed around the end of the 12th century.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="emirate, 1085 CE – 1192 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">emirate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Anatolia and the Levant</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:81.29%;width:2.13%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1085 CE – 1192 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bitlis</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Toghan Arslan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Turkish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Dilmachids, Beylik of Bitlis and Erzen</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1138&amp;focus=dilmacogullari" aria-label="Dilmaçoğulları on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5727 799 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Dilmaçoğulları lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6047 1055L6054 1043"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6054 1043h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6047 1055h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6047 1055h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Bitlis. <a href="/atlas?year=1138&amp;focus=dilmacogullari">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

